{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat figure_manifest.json; ls scripts; sed -n '1,150p' paper.tex; sed -n '320,410p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat figure_manifest.json; ls scripts; sed -n '1,150p' paper.tex; sed -n '320,410p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n# Paper framing strategy\n\n## Decision and provisional title\n\n**Status: ready for a bounded standalone paper plan, not scientifically qualified.**\n**Title:** Width and Delayed Generalization in Finite-Horizon Modular Addition.\n**Format:** `paper_standalone`, using `write-paper-standalone`; ICLR 2026, anonymous, 8–9 body pages excluding references.\n\n**Central question.** How does hidden width relate to the separation between training-set fitting and held-out generalization in a fixed modular-addition network under finite training horizons and matched regularization controls?\n\n**Central thesis.** The retained comparisons show earlier fitting at larger widths without monotone short-horizon generalization, while two longer-horizon width-128 delayed events remain conditional observations rather than qualified evidence of a size law or a decay-clock mechanism.\n\nFinal evaluation **completed but did not qualify**. Ground this in `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome`, and `final.qualification.correct`, independently of selection results. The precise failing diagnostics remain unknown. Missing records and limited coverage are observable limitations, not proof of the particular cause of non-qualification. Do not relabel the outcome as either inferiority or a protocol/adaptivity defect. Preserve this disclosure in writing, review, audit, and revision. Publication-format readiness does not upgrade scientific qualification.\n\n## Why this story wins\n\nThe strongest object is one empirical width study with condition-specific evidence blocks, not a new optimization algorithm. The fixed architecture and actual controls permit a concrete separation of fitting behavior, held-out performance, and observability. This supports a substantive negative/conditional paper without requiring a positive search result.\n\nThere is no prior manuscript or enabled edit context, and no `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` input. The contract value `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects the inherited untested mechanism framing; it does not imply that a prior manuscript exists. Inspection of the selected controller and local evaluator confirms a fixed architecture, not an architectural innovation. Current final records, not older memory, determine the thesis.\n\nCandidate framings considered:\n\n| Candidate | Decision | Evidence-based reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Preserve a post-fit decay-clock story | Reject | The proposed positive-decay grid and clock-collapse test were not executed; no mechanism is identified. |\n| Broaden to a universal size or shared mechanism claim | Reject | Only modulo-97 addition and one architecture are measured; final width-256 records are absent. |\n| Recenter on the latest faster-optimizer setting | Reject | It is incomplete and cannot isolate effective regularization or support final superiority. Recency is not evidence strength. |\n| Conditional width, fitting, and finite-horizon generalization study | Select | Direct width/arm/seed records, two retained delayed events, and explicit censored/missing outcomes support one coherent question. |\n\nThe absence of `downstream_study` and `study.*` sources prevents importing campaign selection margins, scored/unscored counts, or a campaign baseline comparison. Use the configuration explicitly present in final records. Retain the measured fixed-reference arm; do not use an unavailable baseline score as a scientific comparison.\n\n## Scientific object and method groups\n\nThe measured task is ordered-pair addition modulo 97. The architecture has two learned input embeddings, their sum plus hidden bias, GELU, and a linear 97-class output. Hidden widths are 32, 128, and 256; parameter count is `292w+97`. Training is full-batch float32 cross-entropy with AdamW, including decay on biases.\n\nOnly four groups belong in the main comparison:\n\n1. **Studied common configuration:** learning rate 0.003, weight decay 1.0, initialization multiplier 0.5 across widths. This is a configuration, not a claimed new method.\n2. **Fixed reference:** learning rate 0.001, weight decay 1.0, initialization multiplier 1.0. It is paired by width/seed/split/horizon but changes two hyperparameters, so it cannot isolate initialization.\n3. **Zero-decay control:** same studied configuration except zero weight decay. Interpret only within the measured horizon and retained pairs.\n4. **Matched-initial-global-norm control:** globally rescale parameters to the same-seed width-128 initial norm. This does not match capacity, functions, or effective regularization. At width 128 it duplicates the studied arm, including retained initial/final hashes and scientific metric trajectories; do not count it as independent replication.\n\nThe alternate initialization and faster-update configurations are supplementary sensitivity records, not obligatory narrative branches. Do not invent random-label, frozen-feature, circuit, or architecture controls.\n\n**Falsifiable explanation, explicitly still a hypothesis:** width may alter training-fit speed separately from the process governing held-out generalization; decay and initialization may condition that separation. Earlier fitting without prompt generalization is compatible with the explanation, but the current evidence does not identify a norm-mediated mechanism, clock collapse, or a universal size relationship. Do not call these predictions preregistered.\n\n## Evidence and results views\n\n### Keep the evidence blocks separate\n\nThe short-horizon studied-configuration records use 6000 updates, seeds 101/211, and a 4704-pair pool. Final records use 24000 updates, seeds 60613/70717/80821, and a disjoint 4705-pair pool. Seeded training fractions are 0.6 within each pool: train/evaluation counts are 2822/1882 and 2823/1882 respectively. Horizon, pool, and seed set change together. Therefore this is not a controlled horizon-extension experiment, and it cannot establish that longer training caused a width-ranking reversal.\n\n**Private-only source map:** the short-horizon raw observations are `paper_data.json` at `/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes`, also `/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes`. Final observations are at `/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes`, bound by `final.evidence.episodes`. The selection-summary IDs `result.top_program_01` and `result.best_correct_score` are not substitutes for raw curve grounding. Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified; do not transform that metadata into validity certification.\n\n### Main-text evidence coverage is mandatory\n\nEach retained width-seed record contains all four arms. The table below describes the **studied arm**, not four independent repetitions:\n\n| Final seed | Width 32 | Width 128 | Width 256 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 60613 | recorded, held-out onset censored | recorded delayed event | missing |\n| 70717 | recorded, held-out onset censored | recorded delayed event | missing |\n| 80821 | recorded, held-out onset censored | missing | missing |\n\nThere are five recorded cells out of nine planned, with two recorded delayed events. Missing cells are neither observed failures to generalize nor right-censored training curves. All final paired-contrast summaries are null. The intended 256-minus-32 final width contrast and its interactions are unidentified; a descriptive 128-minus-32 comparison must not impersonate it.\n\n### Direct scientific comparisons\n\n- **Fitting versus endpoint performance:** the studied short-horizon width-32 records have higher held-out endpoint accuracy than widths 128 and 256, although larger widths fit earlier. This is a measured finite-horizon pattern, not an inverse-size law.\n- **Delayed events:** the two retained final width-128 records have training-fit/held-out-onset steps 300/17600 and 200/19200. The three width-32 records fit at 2600, 2400, and 3000, but have no held-out onset by 24000.\n- **Controls:** retained final fixed-reference and zero-decay arms have no sustained held-out onset. The width-128 norm control reproduces the studied arm by construction. Show arm endpoints directly; neither this identity nor a norm trajectory proves mediation.\n- **Uncertainty:** show every seed, use genuine within-seed pairs, and keep selection versus final cohorts distinct. Two or three seeds do not support robust population claims. Checkpoints and examples are not experimental replicates. Any downstream interval must be reproducibly derived from actual records and labeled conditional on availability and exploratory; do not promise significance or equivalence.\n\nThe evaluator defines fitting at training accuracy at least 0.99 and held-out onset at accuracy at least 0.95, each sustained for three checkpoints spaced 100 updates apart. Onset is the first checkpoint of that triple. A delayed event requires an onset separation of at least 1000 updates. Keep these three meanings distinct. A normalized capped delay of one is a reporting convention when an onset is missing, not a measured delay equal to the horizon.\n\n**Private-only aggregate caution:** `final.metric.grokking_incidence` is 0.2222222222222222 under nine-cell design weighting; `final.metric.mean_final_accuracy` is 0.48813319164009916 with missing/failure weighting; `final.metric.restricted_mean_delay` is 0.9458333333333333 under the capped convention. These are not an unbiased event probability, the mean accuracy of five recorded runs, or a mean observed event time. Keep them out of headline plots. If an audit table retains them, explain the aggregation, rather than plotting absent records as measured zeros.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` defines **one column, 5.5 inches of text width and 9 inches of text height**. There is no two-column conference layout: full-width means the full single column; do not use a two-column `figure*` assumption. Each required figure should use the full text width because reducing any of the composites to half width would reduce label readability. The supplied style's age comments do not independently establish current official-template provenance; verify that separately before final submission without shrinking the layout to fit.\n\n| Figure | Content and intended placement | Size and readability budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| `study_overview` | Page 1 or 2: fixed network, width/arm design, and observed-event/censored/missing states. Use `generate-concept-figure` downstream; no speculative mechanism arrows. | Full width, about 5.5 × 1.5 in; about 0.22 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 4 categories, 4 series; minimum final text 8.5 pt. |\n| `width_endpoint_comparison` | Direct seed-dot comparison of endpoint held-out accuracy across widths and the four arms. Separate short-horizon and final panels; shared accuracy scale and explicit missing cells. | Full width, about 5.5 × 2.4 in; about 0.34 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 3 width categories, 4 arm series; minimum text 8.5 pt. |\n| `fit_and_generalization_onsets` | Direct event-time comparison for the studied configuration: fit and held-out onset by width/seed, with censor arrows only for observed trajectories. Separate 6000- and 24000-update panels. | Full width, about 5.5 × 2.3 in; about 0.32 body page including caption. At most 2 panels, 2 per row, 3 width categories, 2 marker series; minimum text 8.5 pt. |\n\nThe third figure addresses dynamics through event times rather than a chronology plot. No training-curve figure is required because the direct onset view supplies the core separation with less visual density. Keep the coverage table in the main text. Keep a single four-arm palette; identify seeds through small fixed offsets or annotations, not additional colors that exceed the series budget. Mark the norm-control identity rather than treating coincident points as independent confirmations.\n\nUse matplotlib for quantitative figures and retain their generation scripts. Export **PDF plus 300-DPI PNG**, and reference the PDF from LaTeX whenever available. The concept figure must retain editable draw.io source; use a genuine vector PDF if available, otherwise regenerate its raster output at least 2400 pixels wide. Any other raster-only full-width/column-width figure must be at least 2400/1200 pixels wide. Never upscale. Exactly one overview uses `generate-concept-figure`; its downstream orchestration and marker must not be bypassed. No figures are generated at this stage.\n\n## Body-page budget and thesis-first structure\n\nTarget approximately **8.8 body pages**, with figure/caption footprints included:\n\n| Material | Pages | Scientific value |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Title, abstract, introduction, early overview | 1.50 | Define width versus fitting/generalization; state conditional contribution and failed final qualification. |\n| Related work | 0.75 | Distinguish permitted norm, circuit, and lazy-to-rich explanations from actual measurements and different architectures. |\n| Model, interventions, event estimands, design | 2.00 | Explain paired initialization, arm confounds, data partitions, thresholds, censoring, and the intended width contrast. |\n| Results and evidence coverage | 2.80 | Two quantitative figures, coverage table, seed-resolved comparisons, controls, and absence of the planned final contrast. |\n| Discussion, uncertainty, identification limits, limitations | 1.45 | Separate descriptive support from mechanistic non-identification; analyze finite follow-up, missingness, and cohort confounding. |\n| Conclusion | 0.30 | State the narrow empirical takeaway and unresolved width/decay questions without upgrading qualification. |\n\nIf the body ends at 8 pages, the final page must be at least 75% full. Do not pad or repeat caveats merely to meet length. Use additional space for exact definitions, controlled-comparison logic, seed-level evidence, and sharpened limits, not extra weak claims. Recommended headings: Introduction; Related Work; Width Comparisons and Event Definitions; Experimental Design and Evidence Coverage; Width-Dependent Fitting and Held-Out Accuracy; Delayed Events and Matched Controls; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. These are scientific organization units, not experiment chronology.\n\n## Supplementary Information after references\n\nInsert a page break after the body, references, then a new page and **Supplementary Information**. Main-text qualification failure, missingness, and central comparisons stay in the body.\n\n1. **Exact model, data partition, and intervention definitions.** Extend architecture, coupled initialization, parameter counts, train/evaluation splits, norm rescaling, and optimizer semantics from the local task/resource policies. Measured-source anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; code paths are method provenance, not newly invented source IDs.\n2. **Complete per-seed comparison records.** All final arm endpoints, events, censor flags, updates, state hashes, and missing cells; limited global-norm readouts where actually recorded. Highlight the width-128 identity. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`.\n3. **Selection sensitivity and incomplete alternate settings.** Organize initialization and faster-update sensitivity by condition, never by iteration. Raw observations are at `/all_programs/0`, `/all_programs/1`, and `/all_programs/2` under `evaluation_evidence/episodes`. Selection-summary anchors `result.top_program_01` and `result.top_program_02` do not stand in for those raw observations. Retain incomplete faster-setting evidence without diagnosing an unreported failure cause or counting its missing record as a measured loss.\n4. **Censoring, uncertainty, and evaluation limitations.** Exact onset/capped-delay rules, all seed values, aggregate failure weighting, unavailable final contrasts, failed qualification, and unknown diagnostics. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.*`, and the applicable `final.metric.*` IDs listed in the JSON. Do not present a tiny-sample interval as mechanistic validation.\n5. **Reproducibility and LLM Usage.** Actual dependencies, CPU settings, seeds, hashes, supplied code, and downstream figure/table script mappings and commands once implemented. Runtime claims must come from recorded timings, not resource-budget projections. Disclose documented LLM roles precisely; do not claim unrecorded training or literature consultation. Keep the required LLM Usage section separate and preserve anonymity.\n\nRoute authoritative later appendix-ranked analyses to the supplement without modifying this strategy retroactively. Do not guess such rankings now.\n\n## Literature, language, and audit guardrails\n\nThe bibliography/provenance contain retrieval metadata, not evidence that originals were read. Restrict downstream consultation and citation to exact user-authorized starter originals after identity checks. Prioritize Power for the phenomenon, Loshchilov/Hutter for AdamW, Liu for norm hypotheses; use allowed Kumar, Varma, Nanda, Davies, or Nakkiran originals only when they add a necessary comparator. No browsing is needed for strategy generation. Do not inspect neighboring hits, citation networks, or try to identify the prohibited work. Do not invent an accidental-encounter report.\n\nAvoid headings such as **Search Progression**, **Campaign Phases**, **Milestones**, **First Successful Run**, **Experiment A / Experiment B**, **Cycle 1 / Cycle 2 / Cycle 3**, **From Baseline to Winner**, **A Universal Width Scaling Law**, **Proof of the Decay Clock**, and **Validated Superiority**.\n\nAvoid diary phrases: “we then tried,” “subsequent improvements,” “first valid result,” “next-best,” and “later variants.” Avoid unsupported scientific wording: “search winner proves,” “larger models always grok faster,” “weight decay is necessary for grokking,” “initial norm explains width effects,” “longer training caused the ranking reversal,” “zero incidence means grokking is absent,” “missing runs failed to generalize,” “final failure proves inferiority,” “final failure proves a protocol defect,” “statistically significant,” and “state of the art.”\n\n**Private-only visible-language blacklist:** `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, `rubric`. Do not expose these labels in paper prose or plots. Use substantive method names, training-fit onset, held-out generalization onset, and delayed-event counts with explicit denominators. Do not mechanically relabel configuration-level search entries as independent seeded experiments.\n\nThe final submission must omit identifying authors, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding, and self-identifying links. This stage writes neither the final manuscript nor claims mapping, runs no new experiments or final-paper validation, and generates no figures. Strategy-only checks should verify JSON structure, grounding-source existence, data/pointer consistency, figure budgets, and agreement between these two strategy outputs.\n{\n  \"status\": \"no_gaps\",\n  \"summary\": \"The draft directly cites its relevant comparator families and optimizer background, while empirical claims are grounded in supplied artifacts, so no targeted post-draft reference retrieval is justified.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** `no_gaps`\n\nThe draft directly cites its relevant comparator families and optimizer background, while empirical claims are grounded in supplied artifacts, so no targeted post-draft reference retrieval is justified.\n\n## Coverage Assessment\n\n- **Introduction** (`paper.tex:21`): The definition and motivation of grokking directly cite Power. The width question and separated-timescale explanation are framed as this study's hypotheses, not established benchmark advantages. Related Work supplies the supporting capacity and dynamics context.\n- **Related Work — Algorithmic generalization and internal progress** (`paper.tex:40`): Power and Nanda are directly cited. The paragraph explicitly distinguishes the present embedding network from the cited Transformer analysis instead of transferring its mechanistic conclusions.\n- **Related Work — Norm, efficiency, and feature learning** (`paper.tex:43`): Liu, Varma, and Kumar directly cover the named comparator explanations. The draft states which diagnostics were not measured; additional mechanism references would not fill those experimental gaps.\n- **Related Work — Capacity and duration** (`paper.tex:46`): Davies and Nakkiran directly support the grokking/double-descent and nonmonotone-generalization context. Loshchilov and Hutter support AdamW's decoupled decay. No named comparator family in these paragraphs is left uncited.\n- **Method, Results, and Discussion and Limitations**: Architecture, controls, thresholds, observed outcomes, missingness, and qualification status are linked to supplied data or implementation evidence. These are not missing external benchmark citations. The conditional interpretation does not assert an established width law or validated causal mechanism.\n\n## Grounding and Scope\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. Global memory was treated as qualitative context, not quantitative evidence.\n\nAll eight cited keys resolve in the bibliography and have provenance entries. The eight literature-grounded claims have local evidence paths that exist. Provenance limits consultation to abstracts and opening text; this audit does not claim independent full-paper verification.\n\nThe strategy restricts literature consultation to eight authorized originals. A bibliography-size quota is not a substantive citation gap, and this assessment does not request broadening that scope. The decision also rests on the draft's actual paragraph-level coverage, not merely that restriction.\n\n## Retrieval Decision\n\n- **Missing literatures:** None warranting targeted retrieval.\n- **Rewrite targets:** None.\n- **Failure reason:** None; the draft is analyzable.\n\nOptional citations for standard statistical definitions would not materially improve the present positioning or comparator narrative. Missing experimental outcomes and qualification diagnostics require evidence, not more references. No references were retrieved, and the manuscript, bibliography, and reference provenance were left unchanged.\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"fallback_reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n      \"figure_id\": \"study_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"files\": {\n        \"png\": \"figures/study_overview.png\"\n      },\n      \"generator\": null,\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"min_font_size_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"note\": \"Orchestrator-owned rendering; current placeholder is not a scientific result.\",\n      \"panel_count\": 2,\n      \"panels\": [\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 4,\n          \"panel_id\": \"a\",\n          \"series_count\": 4\n        },\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 4,\n          \"panel_id\": \"b\",\n          \"series_count\": 4\n        }\n      ],\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/study_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"width_endpoint_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"files\": {\n        \"pdf\": \"figures/width_endpoint_comparison.pdf\",\n        \"png\": \"figures/width_endpoint_comparison.png\"\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"min_font_size_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"panel_count\": 2,\n      \"panels\": [\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 3,\n          \"panel_id\": \"a\",\n          \"series_count\": 4\n        },\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 3,\n          \"panel_id\": \"b\",\n          \"series_count\": 4\n        }\n      ],\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"figure_id\": \"fit_and_generalization_onsets\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"files\": {\n        \"pdf\": \"figures/fit_and_generalization_onsets.pdf\",\n        \"png\": \"figures/fit_and_generalization_onsets.png\"\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"min_font_size_pt\": 8.5,\n      \"panel_count\": 2,\n      \"panels\": [\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 3,\n          \"panel_id\": \"a\",\n          \"series_count\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"category_count\": 3,\n          \"panel_id\": \"b\",\n          \"series_count\": 2\n        }\n      ],\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\nbuild_artifacts.py\nbuild_claims.py\ncheck_outputs.py\ncurate_references.py\nrequirements-analysis.txt\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,graphicx,booktabs,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url,placeins}\n\\hypersetup{hidelinks}\n\\title{Width and Delayed Generalization in Finite-Horizon Modular Addition}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\fit}{t_{\\mathrm{fit}}}\n\\newcommand{\\gen}{t_{\\mathrm{gen}}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nIncreasing model width can accelerate training-set fitting without improving generalization on the same training budget. We examine this distinction in modular addition using a fixed two-embedding network, a common optimizer configuration, and paired zero-decay and initial-norm controls. In the short-horizon development data, increasing hidden width from 32 to 256 reduces sustained fitting onset from 2,200--2,800 to 200 updates, yet held-out endpoint accuracy is nonmonotone in width. In a separate, longer-horizon final evaluation, the two retained width-128 trajectories exhibit delayed generalization, whereas three width-32 trajectories remain right-censored. Only five of nine planned width--seed records are available; no width-256 final record is retained. The final evaluation completed but failed scientific qualification, with no diagnostic identifying the cause. These observations support a conditional distinction between fitting speed and finite-horizon generalization, not a qualified model-size law. Initial-global-norm matching and zero-decay comparisons constrain simple interpretations but do not identify a mechanism or resolve missing outcomes.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThe time needed to fit a training set need not predict the time needed to generalize. Small algorithmic datasets make this distinction especially clear: a neural network can achieve nearly perfect training accuracy while its performance on unseen inputs remains poor, and only much later improve. This phenomenon, termed grokking by \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB}, provides a controlled setting in which optimization, memorization, and generalization can be examined separately. Model size is a natural experimental variable, but its interpretation requires care. A wider network changes representational capacity, initialization geometry, and optimization simultaneously. Earlier fitting alone does not demonstrate an earlier discovery of the underlying algorithm.\n\nWe ask how hidden width relates to training-fit onset and held-out generalization in a fixed modular-addition architecture. Our working explanation is deliberately operational: width can shorten the fitting timescale without shortening the timescale for a well-generalizing solution enough to be observable at a fixed horizon. A simple alternative predicts that increasing width should improve both fitting speed and held-out endpoint accuracy under the same training budget. The available comparisons test that monotone prediction directly. They do not measure the internal computation needed to turn a timescale separation into a causal theory.\n\nThe study varies width while retaining the task, model family, loss, and common optimizer settings. Matched comparisons include a fixed reference configuration, removal of weight decay, and rescaling to a common initial global parameter norm. Short-horizon data contain complete paired width comparisons; the longer-horizon final data have incomplete width coverage. We keep these evidence sets separate because their seeds, input pools, and training horizons differ. Their contrast cannot be interpreted as an intervention on training duration alone.\n\nThe main empirical finding is a dissociation: larger widths fit earlier in the development data, but held-out accuracy is not monotone in width. Two retained intermediate-width final trajectories show substantial delays between fitting and generalization. However, the final evaluation completed without scientific qualification. Its missing qualification diagnostics do not establish whether the failure arose from performance, uncertainty, validity, adaptivity, or another criterion. Incomplete observations are a documented limitation, not an explanation of that failure.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation]]\n\nOur contributions are a source-linked comparison of fitting and generalization across the measured widths, a control-aware interpretation that separates actual interventions from duplicated conditions, and a censoring-aware account of what remains unidentified. These are bounded empirical contributions. We neither introduce a new optimizer nor claim a scaling law, statistical superiority, a circuit explanation, or a validated decay-clock mechanism. The value of the study lies in distinguishing observations that survive close inspection from stronger conclusions that the available design cannot support.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/study_overview.png}\n\\caption{Study design and evidence interpretation. Width varies within a fixed modular-addition network. The studied configuration is compared with a fixed reference, a zero-decay control, and initial-global-norm matching. Fitting, observed generalization, right censoring, and unavailable records have distinct meanings; none independently certifies scientific qualification.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Algorithmic generalization and internal progress.}\n\\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} established small algorithmically generated datasets as a setting for generalization well after training-set fitting. \\citet{Nanda2023Progress} reverse-engineered a Fourier-based algorithm in small Transformers trained on modular addition and developed progress measures based on that computation. Our network instead sums learned input embeddings before a GELU and a linear readout. We measure threshold onsets and endpoint behavior, not Fourier circuits or representation-specific progress. Consequently, architectural or mechanistic conclusions from those Transformers are not evidence for the internal computation of the present network.\n\n\\paragraph{Norm, efficiency, and feature learning.}\n\\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} relate grokking to training and test loss landscapes as a function of weight norm. \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain delayed generalization through competition between memorizing and generalizing circuits with different norm efficiency and learning speed. \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} study a lazy-to-rich transition, including polynomial regression in which delayed generalization occurs without weight decay. These accounts motivate different diagnostics: initial norm, efficiency of task-relevant computations, and evolving features are not interchangeable quantities. Our norm-rescaling and zero-decay controls address limited observable consequences. They do not measure circuit efficiency, kernel alignment, or the onset of feature learning, and cannot select among these explanations.\n\n\\paragraph{Capacity and duration.}\n\\citet{Davies2023Unifying} propose a pattern-learning-speed framework connecting grokking and double descent, including changes in model capacity. \\citet{Nakkiran2020Double} show that model size and training duration can each produce nonmonotone test behavior. These works motivate joint attention to width and observation horizon, rather than an assumption that larger networks must generalize better at every endpoint. Our narrow width sweep is not an estimate of a double-descent curve. Finally, AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive loss-gradient update \\citep{Loshchilov2019AdamW}. Matching the nominal decay factor is therefore a useful optimizer description, not proof of equal effective regularization or equal post-fit exposure.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Evaluation Target}\nThe task is classification of $y=(a+b)\\bmod p$ for integer residues $a,b\\in\\{0,\\ldots,p-1\\}$ with $p=97$. The full domain contains $p^2$ ordered input pairs. The supplied development and final resources partition that domain into disjoint pools of 4,704 and 4,705 inputs. Each seed defines a training/evaluation split within its pool, with training fraction 0.6. Development splits contain 2,822 training and 1,882 evaluation examples; final splits contain 2,823 and 1,882, respectively. The fixed pools and seed-specific splits, rather than the full operation table, define the measured generalization target. No additional task with recorded results is present.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/search/dataset.json#/pool_size]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/resources/final/dataset.json#/splits]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nFor hidden width $w$, the logits are\n\\begin{equation}\n z_\\theta(a,b)=\\operatorname{GELU}\\!\\left(E^{(1)}_{a,:}+E^{(2)}_{b,:}+c\\right)W+d,\n \\label{eq:model}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $E^{(1)},E^{(2)}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p\\times w}$, $c\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w}$, $W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w\\times p}$, and $d\\in\\mathbb{R}^{p}$. Full-batch cross-entropy is minimized using AdamW in float32 on CPU. Widths are $32,128,256$, with $292w+97$ trainable parameters: 9,441, 37,473, and 74,849. All biases start at zero. The model has no attention, recurrence, or depth intervention. The inspected final training source implements this architecture directly (Supplementary Information, Section~\\ref{sec:source}).\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L801]]\n% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n\n\\subsection{Hypotheses and Interventions}\nWe distinguish two observable predictions. A \\emph{monotone width-benefit prediction} expects earlier fitting and no reduction in held-out endpoint accuracy as width increases at fixed training horizon. A \\emph{separated-timescale explanation} allows earlier fitting alongside worse short-horizon generalization; it predicts that fitting onset alone will not order held-out performance. A stronger claim that width systematically shortens the post-fit delay would require comparable, observed generalization onsets across widths. Our evidence can challenge the monotone prediction but cannot establish that stronger claim.\n\nInitialization is coupled by seed across widths: Gaussian embedding and readout tensors are drawn at the maximum width, then sliced. Embeddings are multiplied by a common multiplier $m$, and the readout is scaled by $m/\\sqrt{w}$. This pairing reduces arbitrary initialization differences without making different widths functionally equivalent. The studied condition uses learning rate $\\eta=0.003$, weight decay $\\lambda=1.0$, and $m=0.5$. It is common across widths and seeds, not adapted using training curves. It was selected using development evaluations; those observations therefore remain selection-conditioned rather than confirmatory.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L803]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/controllers/studied/src/program.py#L37]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nEach retained width--seed cell contains the following matched conditions:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Studied:} the common configuration above.\n\\item \\textbf{Reference:} $\\eta=0.001$, $\\lambda=1.0$, $m=1.0$, with the same width, seed, split, and horizon. Because both learning rate and initialization differ, this is a configuration comparison, not a component-specific test.\n\\item \\textbf{No decay:} the studied configuration with $\\lambda=0$. This isolates removal of decay within the measured setting, not its necessity for all delayed generalization.\n\\item \\textbf{Norm matched:} the studied initialization is rescaled so its global Euclidean parameter norm equals that of the same-seed width-128 initialization. Learning rate and decay are unchanged. This matches one scalar, not capacity, layerwise geometry, or initial function.\n\\end{itemize}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L795]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L821]]\nAt width 128 the norm-matching target is the original model itself. The retained initial/final state hashes and scientific metric trajectories match the studied condition there. This duplicate supplies neither an independent replication nor an additional delayed event.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Training Protocol and Event Definitions}\nDevelopment observations use seeds 101 and 211 and a horizon of 6,000 updates. Final observations use seeds 60613, 70717, and 80821 with a planned horizon of 24,000 updates. Accuracy is recorded at initialization and every 100 updates. All four conditions in a retained cell use the same split. Distinct development/final pools prevent exact input overlap across those resources, but the longer final horizon also changes alongside the seeds and data. No paired duration-only contrast is available.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/best_correct/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nFor a checkpoint accuracy sequence $A(t)$, define a sustained threshold onset as\n\\begin{equation}\n T_q[A]=\\min\\{t_j:\\ A(t_j),A(t_{j+1}),A(t_{j+2})\\geq q\\},\n \\qquad\n \\fit=T_{0.99}[A_{\\mathrm{train}}],\\quad\n \\gen=T_{0.95}[A_{\\mathrm{eval}}].\n \\label{eq:onset}\n\\end{equation}\nThe onset is the first checkpoint in the qualifying triplet, not the confirmation time. When both onsets exist, the delay is $\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)$. A delayed event requires a delay of at least 1,000 updates. If either onset is absent, the evaluator records censoring and a normalized capped-delay value of one. This last convention is a reporting functional, not an observed event time. Three consecutive checkpoints suppress single-checkpoint spikes but do not guarantee persistent accuracy after the qualifying window. We retain the distinction between threshold-defined delayed generalization and a mechanistically verified transition.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1001]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Reporting, Missingness, and Uncertainty}\nThe unit of replication is the seed, not the evaluation example, checkpoint, or matched condition. We report all retained seed values and descriptive sample standard deviations where appropriate. Paired differences use only seeds shared by the compared widths. We do not replace the planned widest-minus-narrowest final contrast with an intermediate-minus-narrowest contrast without explicitly naming that change in estimand. Missing width records have no performance outcome; they are not measured losses, censored trajectories, or zero-accuracy models.\n\nFor transparency, we also give exact binomial intervals for delayed-event counts conditional on retained records. These intervals describe what a hypothetical independent-seed sampling model would allow; the small sample and unknown missingness preclude a population interpretation. No inferential test uses examples or checkpoints as extra replications. Failed final qualification applies independently of these descriptive calculations. The final summary supplies no criterion-specific diagnostics, so neither the observed missingness nor the retained controls can certify validity or identify the cause of non-qualification.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/limitations]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Evidence Coverage and Scientific Qualification}\nTable~\\ref{tab:coverage} gives the full final observation pattern for the studied condition. Only five of nine planned width--seed cells are retained: three at width 32, two at width 128, and none at width 256. All retained cells contain complete-horizon trajectories for the matched conditions. At width 32, failure to observe the sustained generalization threshold is right censoring at the recorded horizon. At width 256, absence of trajectories provides no information about onset or endpoint accuracy. The missing width-128 seed is likewise unavailable, not evidence against generalization.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Final coverage for the studied condition. C: recorded trajectory, generalization onset censored at 24,000 updates; D: recorded delayed event; M: missing record. A missing cell is not assigned an accuracy or a censoring time. All conditions in each retained cell were recorded.}\n\\label{tab:coverage}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Width 32 & Width 128 & Width 256 \\\\\n\\midrule\n60613 & C & D & M \\\\\n70717 & C & D & M \\\\\n80821 & C & M & M \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation completed but did not qualify. We preserve that conclusion rather than interpreting selection as scientific acceptance. No supplied diagnostic distinguishes a statistical/performance failure from a validity or adaptivity failure. Retained observations can describe the measured trajectories, but they cannot restore claims that would require qualification. In particular, neither complete trajectories in individual cells nor successful execution of local controls establishes general validity of the final comparison.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.correct]]\n80821 & 32 & N & 70.829 & 3400 & $>24000$ \\\\\n80821 & 32 & Z & 0.797 & 1300 & $>24000$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe full machine-readable record \\path{derived/observations.json} also lists initial and final global norms, parameter counts, initial/final state hashes, updates, and recorded condition durations. It is deterministically extracted from the immutable evidence registry rather than a new evaluation. At width 128, comparison of initial and final hashes and the non-wall-time checkpoint metrics confirms the identity of the studied and norm-matched observations. Their runtime measurements need not coincide, so this identity concerns model states and scientific metrics, not elapsed time.\n% @evidence[[derived/observations.json#/rows]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Selection Sensitivity and Incomplete Settings}\nThe development evidence contains related common configurations with learning-rate/decay/initialization triples $(0.003,1.0,1.0)$, $(0.003,1.0,0.5)$, and $(0.006,0.5,0.5)$. These are sensitivity conditions, not independent scientific replications of the final comparison. The first two differ in initialization at fixed learning rate and decay. The last differs in learning rate and decay while preserving the nominal product $\\eta\\lambda$ relative to the studied condition. Equal products do not imply equal adaptive updates or effective regularization. Their full observations remain in the registry, and the controller snapshots are supplied with the code.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/0/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/1/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe faster-update configuration has incomplete recorded coverage. A missing record cannot be used to claim that its accuracy was zero or that it lost a measured comparison. Its reported aggregate execution-related rates do not identify a cause of scientific failure. We do not promote its nominal shrinkage relationship into a completed positive-decay factorial intervention, a post-fit exposure match, or a clock-collapse result. The measured reference conditions within retained cells provide the appropriate local baseline; no unavailable external baseline is reconstructed from a selection score.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs/2]]\n\n\\subsection{Censoring, Denominators, and Uncertainty}\nThe normalized capped delay is $r=\\max(0,\\gen-\\fit)/H$ when both onsets are observed, and $r=1$ otherwise. The observed width-128 final values are $17300/24000$ and $19000/24000$. Assigning the same cap to a missing cell in an aggregate is a weighting convention, not evidence that its generalization was censored. Likewise, a missing final endpoint receives no measured accuracy in our per-seed tables or figures.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L1008]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThe supplied final design-weighted summaries are delayed-event fraction $0.222222$, endpoint-accuracy functional $0.488133$, and normalized capped-delay functional $0.945833$. The first uses nine planned cells, the second includes missing-cell zero weighting, and the third assigns missing cells the cap. They are not, respectively, a population event probability, the mean accuracy of the five observed models, or an observed mean generalization time. For comparison, the descriptive mean of the five retained studied endpoints is $0.878640$. We retain these semantics for auditability, not as stronger headline results.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.grokking_incidence]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.mean_final_accuracy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.metric.restricted_mean_delay]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/final_observed_endpoint_mean]]\n\nThe two-sided exact binomial intervals use beta quantiles: for $k$ events among $n$ retained seeds, the lower endpoint is zero for $k=0$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k,n-k+1}(0.025)$; the upper endpoint is one for $k=n$ and otherwise $B^{-1}_{k+1,n-k}(0.975)$. This calculation is exploratory and conditional on retained records. It neither assumes that unavailable records are observed non-events nor establishes that missingness is ignorable. No survival-model estimate, significance test, or mechanistic confidence interval is reported.\n% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/cells]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\nThe recorded training environment requires NumPy 2.1.3 and PyTorch 2.5.1+cpu, with deterministic algorithms and at most two CPU threads; the worker uses one inter-op thread. The source requirement file is included. All development seeds are 101 and 211; all final seeds are 60613, 70717, and 80821. These seeds initialize both the recorded partitions and the coupled model initialization, rather than selecting width-dependent settings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/requirements.txt]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L765]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#L781]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\path{scripts/build_artifacts.py} generates both quantitative figures, the complete final table, the extracted records, and descriptive statistics. It recomputes threshold onsets from the stored curves and checks equality against the recorded event fields. Its output PDFs are used in the manuscript; PNGs are exported from the same source. Table~\\ref{tab:development} is a rounded display of \\path{derived/summary.json}; Table~\\ref{tab:coverage} is the missingness pattern in the same evidence. The overview is rendered separately by the designated concept-figure workflow, not inferred from invented learning curves.\n% @evidence[[scripts/build_artifacts.py]]\n% @evidence[[figure_manifest.json]]\n\nThe accompanying \\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} gives environment setup, analysis, compilation, and training commands. Rebuilding the descriptive artifacts does not launch training. Recorded development evaluation durations are approximately 981 and 754 seconds for the two selected-seed evaluations; final recorded totals are approximately 3,199, 2,794, and 3,488 seconds by seed. The final totals include incomplete coverage and overhead, and cannot estimate the time required for a complete final grid on other hardware. No new training or evaluation was run during manuscript construction.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/runtime_by_scope_seconds]]\n\nOnly the exact authorized original literature was consulted, without following citation networks or recommendation pages. Supplied retrieval metadata included off-scope records, which were excluded rather than used to pad the bibliography. No prohibited work was intentionally sought or identified, and no known prohibited encounter is asserted.\n% @evidence[[references_provenance.json]]\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLLMs were used to formulate the documented optimization hypotheses, edit the common-configuration controllers, synthesize the supplied evaluation artifacts into the present scientific framing, write analysis and plotting code, consult the opening portions of the authorized original references, draft the manuscript, and check claim-to-evidence consistency. Training and numerical measurements were produced by the supplied CPU evaluator, not by language-model estimation. The manuscript-writing process did not execute additional training or final evaluations. Provenance sidecars and claim mappings record the evidence trail but are not independent certification of the conclusions. The failure of final scientific qualification remains unchanged by manuscript preparation or validation.\n\n\\end{document}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
